            "text": "\"To find the maximum number of members that can have 3 pets, we can set up the following steps:\n\n1. Let $x$ be the number of family members who have 3 pets.\n2. There are 20 family members in total, so the remaining number of members is $20 - x$.\n3. Since each member must have at least one pet, the remaining $20 - x$ members must each have at least 1 pet to minimize the total number of pets used by the rest of the family.\n4. Setting up an inequality for the total number of pets:\n   $$\\text{Total pets} \\ge 3x + 1 \\cdot (20 - x)$$\n   $$32 \\ge 2x + 20$$\n5. Subtract 20 from both sides:\n   $$12 \\ge 2x$$\n6. Divide by 2:\n   $$x \\le 6$$\n\nIf 6 members have 3 pets, they have $6 \\times 3 = 18$ pets. The remaining $20 - 6 = 14$ members can each have 1 pet, accounting for $14 \\times 1 = 14$ pets. In total, this gives $18 + 14 = 32$ pets, which satisfies all conditions.\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of members that can have 3 pets is 6.\"",
